1
00:00:00,030 --> 00:00:02,358
Can a 27-billion-parameter
model fit

2
00:00:02,378 --> 00:00:04,092
on a plain 8 GB graphics card?

3
00:00:04,179 --> 00:00:04,979
Well, actually, yes.

4
00:00:05,037 --> 00:00:06,922
On an 8 GB card,
you change a single file,

5
00:00:06,942 --> 00:00:10,359
and Qwen 27B goes from 3.2
to 18 tokens per second.

6
00:00:10,537 --> 00:00:13,802
Except this measurement comes from
a GTX 1080 Ti limited to 8 GB.

7
00:00:13,938 --> 00:00:14,747
So, the real question:

8
00:00:14,851 --> 00:00:16,807
what does this file make you lose
in exchange?

9
00:00:17,227 --> 00:00:19,541
In this video, we'll look at
how this file does everything

10
00:00:19,561 --> 00:00:20,784
to fit in 8 GB,

11
00:00:20,846 --> 00:00:22,544
what it costs you
in conversation memory,

12
00:00:22,564 --> 00:00:23,854
and what it costs you in quality.

13
00:00:24,041 --> 00:00:25,402
And at the end, you'll see a job

14
00:00:25,422 --> 00:00:27,287
that this file fails almost
9 times out of 10,

15
00:00:27,359 --> 00:00:28,942
in the tests of the very people
who made it.

16
00:00:29,399 --> 00:00:30,515
First, what it really changes.

17
00:00:30,655 --> 00:00:32,126
Take a 500-token answer.

18
00:00:32,360 --> 00:00:35,175
At 3.2 tokens per second,
you wait 2 minutes 36 seconds.

19
00:00:35,339 --> 00:00:36,602
At 18, it's 28 seconds.

20
00:00:36,790 --> 00:00:39,282
It's just a division,
but it gives you the idea.

21
00:00:39,463 --> 00:00:42,148
So this gap changes
how you talk to AI.

22
00:00:42,332 --> 00:00:44,148
When every answer takes
two and a half minutes,

23
00:00:44,168 --> 00:00:45,700
you don't dare ask it anything anymore.

24
00:00:45,813 --> 00:00:48,103
You write one big block to avoid
going back and forth.

25
00:00:48,202 --> 00:00:49,953
At 28 seconds, you can
follow up, correct,

26
00:00:49,998 --> 00:00:52,268
and go back and forth
without worrying about what's going on.

27
00:00:52,564 --> 00:00:54,342
But this gain only affects
the writing.

28
00:00:54,461 --> 00:00:56,324
Before writing,
the model reads your request,

29
00:00:56,344 --> 00:00:59,191
and that reading stays about the same
with both files.

30
00:00:59,272 --> 00:01:00,725
And if it thinks before answering,

31
00:01:00,745 --> 00:01:03,115
that thinking is also
tokens to write.

32
00:01:03,236 --> 00:01:06,013
A short answer on screen can therefore
hide a lot more work

33
00:01:06,033 --> 00:01:06,880
than you think.

34
00:01:07,184 --> 00:01:08,122
So where does this gain come from?

35
00:01:08,447 --> 00:01:09,967
The old file
didn't fit on the card.

36
00:01:09,987 --> 00:01:12,783
Part of it ran in the PC's RAM,
with the processor,

37
00:01:12,893 --> 00:01:14,403
and the card spent its time
waiting for it.

38
00:01:14,424 --> 00:01:16,364
Basically, a cook
with half of his ingredients

39
00:01:16,384 --> 00:01:17,559
in another room:

40
00:01:17,579 --> 00:01:18,379
he can be as fast as he likes,

41
00:01:18,399 --> 00:01:20,929
but he spends his time running around
to fetch what he's missing.

42
00:01:20,995 --> 00:01:23,585
Well, the new small file, though,
fits entirely in the card.

43
00:01:23,652 --> 00:01:25,685
It's called UD-IQ2_XS.

44
00:01:26,001 --> 00:01:27,733
To me, it's gibberish,
but that's its name.

45
00:01:27,847 --> 00:01:31,614
It's 7.63 GB, and it was published
by Unsloth on November 11.

46
00:01:31,891 --> 00:01:33,801
I'll show you the full name,
it's much simpler.

47
00:01:34,181 --> 00:01:35,818
But to fit
on an 8 GB card,

48
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:38,108
the model had to go on
an incredible diet.

49
00:01:38,253 --> 00:01:40,028
Its numbers are stored
with less precision,

50
00:01:40,048 --> 00:01:41,598
that's what we call quantization.

51
00:01:41,738 --> 00:01:44,135
The model keeps its name,
but it doesn't answer quite the same.

52
00:01:44,588 --> 00:01:47,198
By the way, the MTP, the module
that doubled the speed

53
00:01:47,376 --> 00:01:49,109
in the previous video,
is left out

54
00:01:49,129 --> 00:01:50,569
of this measurement, for lack of room.

55
00:01:50,720 --> 00:01:52,971
So the 18 tokens per second we got
are without it.

56
00:01:53,385 --> 00:01:55,595
Okay, we've found everything we gain
with this file.

57
00:01:55,724 --> 00:01:56,959
Now, what it costs us,

58
00:01:57,093 --> 00:01:58,793
and the first price is space.

59
00:01:58,905 --> 00:02:01,613
The file is 7.63 GB,
and your card has 8.

60
00:02:01,775 --> 00:02:04,701
But the model isn't the only thing
that goes in your card.

61
00:02:04,847 --> 00:02:07,589
The engine also needs room to work.
Your desktop, for example:

62
00:02:07,609 --> 00:02:10,071
if you plug your monitor directly
into your graphics card,

63
00:02:10,091 --> 00:02:11,082
it needs some too.

64
00:02:11,102 --> 00:02:13,485
And above all, you have to keep
the conversation somewhere.

65
00:02:13,505 --> 00:02:15,106
It also needs
that same card.

66
00:02:15,192 --> 00:02:17,936
So the file size doesn't tell you
how much discussion you have left.

67
00:02:18,222 --> 00:02:20,852
And the conversation's memory,
that is, the KV cache:

68
00:02:20,904 --> 00:02:24,596
in the measurement, with an 8-bit cache,
about 14,000 tokens are left

69
00:02:24,616 --> 00:02:25,416
for the discussion.

70
00:02:25,511 --> 00:02:28,580
Your request and everything
the model writes, its thinking included,

71
00:02:28,600 --> 00:02:29,752
so they share this space.

72
00:02:29,835 --> 00:02:31,167
For chatting, that's fine,

73
00:02:31,198 --> 00:02:33,255
but pasting a long document
or a long prompt

74
00:02:33,275 --> 00:02:34,497
is much harder.

75
00:02:34,722 --> 00:02:36,623
And the good news here
is that the speed holds up.

76
00:02:36,643 --> 00:02:38,091
With almost 14,000 tokens in it,

77
00:02:38,111 --> 00:02:40,601
the writing is still at
15 tokens per second.

78
00:02:40,744 --> 00:02:43,378
You can also compress
this cache to 4 bits, for example.

79
00:02:43,427 --> 00:02:46,990
There, the person who measured goes up
to about 22,500 tokens.

80
00:02:47,010 --> 00:02:48,649
But with a full
4-bit cache,

81
00:02:48,669 --> 00:02:51,123
the writing drops to around
12 tokens per second.

82
00:02:51,166 --> 00:02:52,345
It's another comfort setting,

83
00:02:52,365 --> 00:02:55,085
but it has its cost too,
on the final bill.

84
00:02:55,180 --> 00:02:58,732
And when he filled the memory
beyond 24,000 tokens, it crashed.

85
00:02:58,820 --> 00:03:00,078
The model loads perfectly,

86
00:03:00,098 --> 00:03:02,325
but it's the conversation that ends up
no longer fitting.

87
00:03:02,565 --> 00:03:03,622
For short exchanges,

88
00:03:03,642 --> 00:03:05,875
everyday requests,
you don't see the limit.

89
00:03:06,010 --> 00:03:07,024
But for a long session,

90
00:03:07,044 --> 00:03:09,373
for example for code
with files and a history,

91
00:03:09,393 --> 00:03:10,755
that's where it starts to get stuck.

92
00:03:11,178 --> 00:03:14,136
And if this video helped you
make the right choice for your card,

93
00:03:14,176 --> 00:03:15,146
don't forget to subscribe.

94
00:03:15,428 --> 00:03:16,980
Okay, the second price is quality.

95
00:03:17,168 --> 00:03:18,819
And here, the numbers come from Unsloth
themselves,

96
00:03:18,895 --> 00:03:21,667
it's their own test campaign,
not an independent check.

97
00:03:21,726 --> 00:03:24,121
And their tests run with
a very long conversation

98
00:03:24,141 --> 00:03:25,734
and thinking pushed to the maximum:

99
00:03:25,844 --> 00:03:27,791
nothing like an 8 GB card.

100
00:03:27,955 --> 00:03:29,331
These scores show where it breaks,

101
00:03:29,378 --> 00:03:31,409
but they don't give you
your own success rate.

102
00:03:31,718 --> 00:03:34,538
On LiveCodeBench, for example,
programming exercises,

103
00:03:34,558 --> 00:03:37,276
the full model, with no compression,
is around 89,

104
00:03:37,296 --> 00:03:38,966
the small file around 85.

105
00:03:39,039 --> 00:03:41,421
Honestly, for a model
compressed this much, it holds up well.

106
00:03:41,508 --> 00:03:42,945
So, what do you think,
does it hold up too

107
00:03:42,965 --> 00:03:44,536
when you hand it a real project?

108
00:03:44,920 --> 00:03:48,326
On their agent test, where the model
works alone in a code repository,

109
00:03:48,409 --> 00:03:51,116
the full model succeeds
on about 40% of tasks,

110
00:03:51,136 --> 00:03:52,692
the small file, about 13%.

111
00:03:52,912 --> 00:03:54,222
Here, honestly, it plummets.

112
00:03:54,266 --> 00:03:56,975
And the old file, the one that ran
at 3.2 tokens per second,

113
00:03:56,995 --> 00:03:59,400
stays around 36%.
So, on this kind of work,

114
00:03:59,420 --> 00:04:02,736
the small file succeeds 3 times
less often than the full model.

115
00:04:02,998 --> 00:04:05,001
And actually, when you ask it
for help with a function,

116
00:04:05,197 --> 00:04:06,983
you read its answer
and judge it yourself.

117
00:04:07,112 --> 00:04:08,869
An agent, though, chains
decisions on its own,

118
00:04:08,889 --> 00:04:10,185
and it modifies several files,

119
00:04:10,205 --> 00:04:13,385
and a mistake at the start always
gets paid for at every step after.

120
00:04:13,616 --> 00:04:15,584
So the counter from the start
is no longer enough.

121
00:04:15,645 --> 00:04:17,932
So, an answer in 28 seconds
that you have to repair

122
00:04:17,952 --> 00:04:19,746
can cost you more
than a slow answer

123
00:04:19,766 --> 00:04:20,876
that works the first time.

124
00:04:21,144 --> 00:04:22,751
And if you're on LM Studio, for example,

125
00:04:22,771 --> 00:04:24,500
its documentation has the same settings:

126
00:04:24,622 --> 00:04:25,926
how much of the model goes on the card,

127
00:04:25,946 --> 00:04:27,056
the conversation size,

128
00:04:27,076 --> 00:04:28,812
and the 8-bit or 4-bit cache.

129
00:04:28,945 --> 00:04:31,615
Except all these speeds
were measured in llama.cpp,

130
00:04:31,696 --> 00:04:32,628
not in LM Studio.

131
00:04:32,765 --> 00:04:34,327
And the option to turn off thinking

132
00:04:34,347 --> 00:04:36,556
depends on how the model
is configured too.

133
00:04:36,826 --> 00:04:38,976
So what can we conclude
about this file, in the end?

134
00:04:39,041 --> 00:04:41,117
Actually, for chatting
and having something explained,

135
00:04:41,137 --> 00:04:42,107
it's the right file,

136
00:04:42,178 --> 00:04:44,819
since the model fits
entirely in 8 GB,

137
00:04:44,850 --> 00:04:46,481
and it writes over 5 times faster.

138
00:04:46,589 --> 00:04:48,928
As long as the conversation stays short,
the space is enough.

139
00:04:48,948 --> 00:04:52,314
To get help with a piece of code
or fix a small script, for example,

140
00:04:52,334 --> 00:04:54,524
in their tests, the programming
exercises hold up well.

141
00:04:54,544 --> 00:04:56,348
But you really have to
reread everything yourself,

142
00:04:56,368 --> 00:04:57,947
and if you don't give it
your whole project at once,

143
00:04:57,967 --> 00:04:58,826
it could work.

144
00:04:58,920 --> 00:05:01,252
For an agent that works, though,
alone on a project,

145
00:05:01,285 --> 00:05:02,239
keep the old file,

146
00:05:02,259 --> 00:05:04,698
even if you have to wait
at 3.2 tokens per second,

147
00:05:04,842 --> 00:05:05,697
because in the test,

148
00:05:05,717 --> 00:05:08,013
the small file fails almost
9 times out of 10.

149
00:05:08,211 --> 00:05:09,786
The old one, about 2 times out of 3.

150
00:05:09,933 --> 00:05:11,204
So what's the point of going fast

151
00:05:11,224 --> 00:05:13,135
if it's to get it wrong at every step?

152
00:05:13,420 --> 00:05:15,460
So, what this small file makes you
gain or lose,

153
00:05:15,480 --> 00:05:16,468
you know it now.

154
00:05:16,568 --> 00:05:19,020
For chatting, with a bit of room,
there's no problem,

155
00:05:19,040 --> 00:05:19,964
it's top quality.

156
00:05:19,984 --> 00:05:21,598
But for an agent that's going
to perform tasks

157
00:05:21,616 --> 00:05:23,194
much longer than that,
that's where it gets tricky.

158
00:05:23,214 --> 00:05:25,959
So, on the same card, really,
you can do 2 different jobs

159
00:05:25,979 --> 00:05:27,429
depending on the file you give it.

160
00:05:27,663 --> 00:05:28,772
So, tell me in the comments:

161
00:05:28,792 --> 00:05:31,238
are you ready to use
a different file

162
00:05:31,258 --> 00:05:32,738
depending on what you have to do,

163
00:05:32,758 --> 00:05:35,415
or depending on what you want to do,
according to your needs?

164
00:05:35,499 --> 00:05:37,759
And if you liked this video,
don't hesitate to subscribe,

165
00:05:37,779 --> 00:05:40,203
to share the video
and to boost the channel.

166
00:05:40,223 --> 00:05:41,149
It'll make me very happy.

167
00:05:41,169 --> 00:05:42,865
Well, see you next time. Bye!
